      <gmd:abstract>
        <gco:CharacterString>Data was mined from published and agency research projects conducted between 2015-2025 on Prince William Sound beaches. This data was utilized to develop a listing methodology (LM). A LM is systematic method for evaluating attainment of Clean Water Act approved water quality standards. Five tiers of data analysis were developed. The tiers are 1) test pit data, 2) sediment chemistry data, 3) biological data, 4) modeled data, and 5) overwhelming evidence. For each tier thresholds of water quality standards attainment were developed. The Alaska Department of Environmental Conservation currently has 36 beach segments listed as water quality standards impaired. Utilizing the method created and redrawing beach segments to reflect geomorphic features results in 5 remaining proposed impairments. The archived documents consist of 1) Listing Methodology, 2) Technical Approach to the development of the LM, 3) geodatabase of data synthesized from existing research and draft attainment analysis (EVOS_2026_Lingering_Oil_Status.csv), 4) a narrative of the draft attainment analysis (Public-Notice-Version-26IR-cat-4b-EVOS.pdf), and 5) GIS package description of geodatabase. All material will undergo a public comment period in the spring of 2026.</gco:CharacterString>
      </gmd:abstract>
      <gmd:purpose>
        <gco:CharacterString>This project utilizes existing data from research conducted since the original spill to create a methodology for assessing attainment of EVOS impacted beach against Alaska water quality standards. Accurately reflecting the areas of impairment is crucial for making informed management decisions, setting priorities, and ensures public transparency.</gco:CharacterString>
      </gmd:purpose>
