      <gmd:lineage>
        <gmd:LI_Lineage>
          <gmd:statement>
            <gco:CharacterString>An annotated bibliography was completed to identify relevant research. Data was mined from selected research. A tiered approach for data assessment was developed: 1) test pit data, 2) sediment chemistry data, 3) biological data, 4) modeled data, and 5) overwhelming evidence. It begins with Tier 1. If sufficient data are available to complete an assessment, then no further assessment occurs. If sufficient data are not available, then proceed to Tier 2. This process repeats through Tier 5. This approach prioritizes empirical data less than 10 years old while allowing for other lines of evidence to still be considered in Tier 5. For example, if test pit data from 1989 to 1991 for an oiled beach were available as well as sediment chemistry data from 2021, the sediment chemistry data would take precedence because it is more recent (within the last 10 years) and thus more likely to reflect the current conditions of that area. Data from selected research was collected and compiled into a geodatabase. This data was then analyzed used python scripts. Assessments based on the results of the python scripts were added as a layer to the geodatabase.</gco:CharacterString>
          </gmd:statement>
          <gmd:processStep>
            <gmd:LI_ProcessStep>
              <gmd:description>
                <gco:CharacterString>No original data was collected. Existing data was digitized and analyzed using python scripts.</gco:CharacterString>
              </gmd:description>
            </gmd:LI_ProcessStep>
          </gmd:processStep>
          <gmd:source>
